//! WAITER — §2.2's generic wait aggregator, and the decode of the record the
//! kernel copies back (design 178 D5).
//!
//! It is the TOP of the waitable stack rather than the bottom of it: `add` is
//! one overload per kind, so this file names `Event`, `Interrupt` and `Timer`
//! and each of those names nothing here. That is the whole reason it moved
//! above them in the declared order — the brief's sketch had it below, which
//! the three overloads make impossible.
//!
//! **AND THE FOURTH KIND IS WHERE THAT SHAPE RAN OUT** (sawos design 8, M3
//! unit 5.5). A PROCESS handle is waitable too, but `Process` is declared
//! ABOVE this file (in `sos.system`, with `System` and `BootHandle`, because
//! every pair of those three names the other), so the fourth `add` overload
//! written HERE would import downward into its own importer. It lives in
//! `sos.system` instead, as an `extension Waiter` beside `Process` — same op,
//! same rules — which callers see because extension lookup follows the
//! facade's `public import` since sawlang 0.2.0 (the first pin bump; SL-7's
//! resolution; unit 5.5 shipped `Process.attach` in the interim).
//!
//! **AND M4 UNIT 3 SENT FOUR MORE THE SAME WAY** (sawos design 15). The four
//! PIPE kinds are §2.2 waitables now, and their `add`/`give` overloads are
//! declared in `sos.pipe` for the same reason the Process one is declared in
//! `sos.system`: that file names `Waiter` (its four overloads take one), so it
//! sits ABOVE this one and an overload written here would be the
//! cycle again. What this file still declares is `AttachMode`, the three
//! overloads whose types it names, and the DECODE — which is where the four new
//! payload cases land, because the record is one thing whatever wrote it.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, WaiterHandle, NO_HANDLE, WaitTag, WaitResult,
               WaitPayload, WaitBuffer, PipeMsg, ReplyDelivery, ReplyLevel,
               InletLevel, OutletLevel, EventHandle, InterruptHandle,
               TimerHandle, PIPE_BODY_BYTES,
               WAIT_HEADER_WORDS, WAIT_KEY_WORD, WAIT_TAG_WORD,
               WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD, WAIT_BODY_LEN_WORD,
               WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING, WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT, wait_record_bytes}

import sos.floor.{checked, sos_handle_release, sos_waiter_add,
                  sos_waiter_remove, sos_waiter_wait}
import sos.event.{Event}
import sos.interrupt.{Interrupt}
import sos.timer.{Timer}

/// **WHAT AN ATTACHMENT DOES AT DELIVERY** (`designs/010` ruling 9(b); sawos
/// design 15, M4 unit 3).
///
/// One-shot is a property of the ATTACHMENT — the attacher's choice, like the
/// key — so it is a defaulted trailing parameter on every `add` rather than a
/// second method per kind, which is the `EventMode` doctrine and is what keeps
/// the existing call sites unchanged.
///
/// **IT IS NOT THE OTHER AXIS.** Whether the attach CONSUMES the caller's handle
/// is spelled by a different VERB (`give`), not by a third case here: consuming
/// changes what the call does to its argument, and ruling 4's own mode-enum
/// rejection applies — a runtime flag can change neither a return type nor a Saw
/// function's effect. The two are a 2x2, and ruling 11(c) struck
/// `OneShotConsuming` for exactly that reason.
public enum AttachMode: UInt {
    /// Stay subscribed. The attachment lives until `remove(key)` or until the
    /// Waiter does — §2.2's ratified default, now said out loud.
    case Persistent = 0,
    /// Detach when a record for this key is COPIED OUT — never at mere
    /// readiness. What it buys: a blocked poster's room-wait, a
    /// request-abandoned watcher and a wait-once death supervisor all stop
    /// owing a trailing `remove`.
    case OneShot = 1,
}

/// The flag bit `mode` becomes at the wire. A `match` rather than a cast: the
/// enum's numbers are the typed surface's own and the wire's are `sosabi`'s, and
/// the vDSO discipline is that the two are allowed to differ.
public(package) func mode_flag(mode: AttachMode) -> UInt {
    match mode {
        case Persistent -> 0,
        case OneShot -> WAITER_ADD_ONESHOT,
    }
}

/// Decode a record the kernel copied out into `WaitResult`.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN TAG PANICS, loudly, and that is deliberate. Every other decode in
/// this package treats its input as data a process might legitimately be handed;
/// this one does not, because the KERNEL wrote it out of the same `WaitTag`
/// declaration this function reads. A tag that does not decode is therefore a
/// skew between the kernel and the `sos` module it exports — a bug in the pair,
/// not a runtime condition — and the never-hide rule says a bug the caller
/// cannot act on should stop the program where it happened rather than become
/// an `Err` somebody maps to "unknown".
///
/// **THE SAME RULE COVERS THE PAYLOAD'S OWN SUB-ENUMS** (sawos design 15). An
/// `OutletLevel`, an `InletLevel` and a `ReplyLevel` all arrive in the payload
/// word out of declarations the kernel wrote from, so a value that does not
/// decode is the same kernel/`sos` skew a bad tag is, and it panics on the same
/// terms rather than becoming a case somebody has to invent a meaning for.
///
/// **AND IT TAKES THE BUFFER RATHER THAN THREE WORDS SINCE M4 UNIT 3**: a reply
/// delivery's body lives in the buffer's body region, so the decode has to be
/// able to reach it. Everything else it reads is still the header's words.
///
/// `public(package)` since M4 unit 3.5 (sawos design 17), for `attach`'s own
/// reason: `PipeRequest.reply_wait` is a SECOND door onto the same delivery, and
/// it is declared in `sos.pipe` because that is where its receiver's type lives.
/// One record, one decode — a second copy of this match would be the one place
/// a kernel/`sos` skew could hide.
public(package) func decode_wait(buf: &WaitBuffer) -> WaitResult {
    let key = buf.header[WAIT_KEY_WORD]
    let tag = buf.header[WAIT_TAG_WORD]
    let payload = buf.header[WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD]
    guard let kind = WaitTag.from(raw: tag) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote wait tag {}, which this build cannot name", tag)
    }
    match kind {
        case Event -> WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Event(word: payload)),
        case Interrupt -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                     what: WaitPayload.Interrupt(line: payload)),
        case Timer -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                 what: WaitPayload.Timer(fires: payload)),
        // THE FOURTH TAG (sawos design 8, M3 unit 5.5): a process ended, and
        // the payload is §8's status word — the same bits `Process.get_status`
        // answers with, so a supervisor parses one thing however it learned.
        case Process -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                   what: WaitPayload.Process(status: payload)),
        // **THE FOUR PIPE TAGS ARE M4 UNIT 3's** (sawos design 15). Three decode
        // a level word; the fourth is the one that reaches into the body.
        case Readable -> {
            guard let level = OutletLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote outlet level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Readable(state: level))
        },
        case Room -> {
            guard let level = InletLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote inlet level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Room(state: level))
        },
        case Reply -> {
            guard let level = ReplyLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote reply level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Reply(outcome: reply_of(level, &buf)))
        },
        // NOTHING TO DECODE, and that is the case rather than an omission: the
        // fact IS the notification, so the payload word carries no information
        // and `WaitPayload.Abandoned` binds nothing.
        case Abandoned -> WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Abandoned),
    }
}

/// The reply half of the decode, split out because it is the only arm that
/// builds an owning value.
///
/// **THE BODY IS COPIED OUT OF THE FRAME BUFFER HERE, ONCE.** `PipeMsg` owns its
/// bytes so that the delivery outlives the buffer it arrived in — which is what
/// makes `wait()` an ordinary value-returning call rather than one that hands
/// back a borrow of its own frame.
func reply_of(level: ReplyLevel, buf: &WaitBuffer) -> ReplyDelivery {
    match level {
        case Data -> ReplyDelivery.Data(
            msg: PipeMsg(len: buf.header[WAIT_BODY_LEN_WORD], bytes: buf.body)),
        case PeerClosed -> ReplyDelivery.PeerClosed,
    }
}

/// A handle to a Waiter — §2.2's generic wait aggregator, epoll/Port-style.
///
/// Attachments are PERSISTENT BY DEFAULT: `add` once, and the handle stays
/// watched until `remove`. `AttachMode.OneShot` is the opt-in that detaches at
/// the delivery instead (M4 unit 3, `designs/010` ruling 9(b)), and readiness is
/// LEVEL-TRIGGERED either way, so a signal that lands before anyone waits is
/// still there when the wait arrives.
///
/// **THE ONE-SHOT FOOTGUN IS STATED RATHER THAN GUARDED**: attach one-shot, take
/// the delivery, and `wait()` again on a Waiter with nothing left attached, and
/// the thread parks forever — which the kernel's deadlock report names, exactly
/// as it always has for an empty Waiter. A subscription that ends itself is a
/// subscription you have to re-arm.
///
/// Several threads may park on one Waiter (§2.2), and a ready handle wakes one
/// of them; a handle attaches to at most one Waiter, and attaching it twice
/// terminates the process, on the faults ruling's terms — it is a mistake only
/// the attacher could have made.
/// **`NoCopy`, AND DROPPING IT RELEASES THE HANDLE** (sawos design 3 D-5).
/// Attachments belong to the WAITER object, not to the handle, so a released
/// handle detaches nothing — the subscriptions live until the object does, which
/// is until the process's teardown.
public struct Waiter {
    public(package) handle: WaiterHandle,
}

extension Waiter: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension Waiter {
    /// Attach `event` under `key`, which `wait` hands back when it becomes
    /// ready. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` here and `EventRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// KEYS ARE UNIQUE PER WAITER: `key` must not already name an attachment on
    /// this Waiter, and reusing one TERMINATES the process. It is the invariant
    /// `remove` and the wait answer both rest on — with two attachments under
    /// one key, neither could say which it meant.
    ///
    /// IT TAKES A `&Event` AND NOT A HANDLE WORD. That is the module
    /// docstring's rule reaching cross-type plumbing: there is no way to spell
    /// a handle you were not given, so an attachment names an object this
    /// process holds by construction. The field access below is same-PACKAGE —
    /// design 5 made each waitable's `handle` `public(package)` so this
    /// module can read a sibling's — which is why no accessor is owed and
    /// none exists.
    ///
    /// ONE METHOD PER WAITABLE KIND, and it stays that way on purpose — see the
    /// brief's note on why a `Waitable` trait is not the shape (a public trait
    /// with a handle-producing requirement is a forged-handle door under the
    /// orphan rule, and Saw has no sealed traits yet). Kinds are a LIST, so
    /// overloads are a list; it is MODES that multiply, which is why
    /// `event_create` takes its mode as a value.
    ///
    /// The second kind is here now, and it is what the shape predicted: each
    /// overload is TWO LINES delegating to one internal, so the list grows by a
    /// method per kind and the wire call has one caller.
    public func add(&self, event: &Event, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(event.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// Attach an Interrupt under `key`, which `wait` hands back with the line
    /// when it fires. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` here and
    /// `InterruptRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// Every rule the Event overload states holds unchanged: keys are unique
    /// per Waiter, a handle attaches to at most one Waiter, and readiness is
    /// level-triggered — so an Interrupt that fired BEFORE it was attached is
    /// reported by this call, which is the ordering a driver hits when the
    /// device was already asserting at bring-up.
    public func add(&self, interrupt: &Interrupt, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(interrupt.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// Attach a Timer under `key`, which `wait` hands back with the FIRE COUNT
    /// when it comes due. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach` here and
    /// `TimerRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// The third kind, and every rule the other two state holds unchanged: keys
    /// are unique per Waiter, a handle attaches to at most one Waiter, and
    /// readiness is level-triggered — so a Timer that fired BEFORE it was
    /// attached is reported by this call rather than lost.
    ///
    /// A DISARMED Timer is simply never ready, so attaching one and waiting
    /// parks until somebody arms it. That is what makes "arm, then wait" and
    /// "attach, then arm" both work.
    public func add(&self, timer: &Timer, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(timer.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    // **THE FOURTH KIND IS NOT HERE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE THAT IS NOT**
    // (sawos design 8, M3 unit 5.5; respelled at the first pin bump). A
    // PROCESS handle attaches like the three above — same op, same key rules,
    // same level-triggered contract — but the overload is declared in
    // `sos.system` where `Process` is: this module sits BELOW that one (`add`
    // reads its argument's handle field), so an overload naming `Process`
    // here would be the import cycle DF-232e diagnoses, exactly as `Waiter`
    // below the three waitables would have been. Since sawlang 0.2.0 an
    // extension travels with `public import`, so the split is invisible at
    // the call site — `waiter.add(process:, key:)` reads as the fourth
    // member of this list, which is what design 8 ruled.

    /// The one call the per-kind overloads share. `public(package)` and not
    /// `public` — a raw handle word crosses here and nowhere else, and the
    /// module docstring's no-forged-handles rule rests on this form being
    /// unreachable from USER code. Package width exists for exactly one
    /// caller: the fourth overload, in `sos.system`, whose receiver type
    /// cannot be named here (the header says why).
    public(package) func attach(&self, target: UInt, key: UInt,
                                flags: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_waiter_add(self.handle, target, key, flags))
    }

    /// **THE CONSUMING ATTACH'S ONE CALL, shared by every `give` overload**
    /// (`designs/010` ruling 11(c)). `attach` with the consuming bit set, which
    /// is the whole difference at the wire; what makes `give` a different VERB
    /// rather than a different argument is what it does to the caller's value,
    /// and that is spelled in each overload's signature.
    ///
    /// `public(package)` for `attach`'s own reason: the `give` overloads for the
    /// kinds declared above this file live beside those kinds, so they call in
    /// from `sos.pipe` and `sos.system`.
    public(package) func attach_consuming(&self, target: UInt, key: UInt,
                                          mode: AttachMode) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(target, key, mode_flag(mode) | WAITER_ADD_CONSUMING)
    }

    /// **HAND AN EVENT TO THIS WAITER, AND STOP HOLDING IT** (`designs/010`
    /// ruling 11(c); sawos design 15, M4 unit 3).
    ///
    /// **`give` IS THE TREE'S ONE VERB FOR "OWNERSHIP LEAVES THROUGH THIS
    /// RECEIVER"** (user-named, Sep 1). `Process.give` hands a handle to a
    /// process; `Waiter.give` hands it to a SUBSCRIPTION. A distinct name rather
    /// than a by-value overload of `add`, so the consuming semantic is visible
    /// at the call site and overload resolution never hinges on a sigil.
    ///
    /// **SAME VERB, DIFFERENT GATE**, and it is worth saying at the op:
    /// give-to-PROCESS crosses a process boundary and spends the `Transfer`
    /// right; give-to-WAITER crosses none — the attachment lives in this
    /// process's own Waiter — so it spends the `Wait` right alone, exactly as
    /// `add` does.
    ///
    /// **THE REFUSAL HANDS THE OBJECT BACK** (user-ruled, Sep 1): a full
    /// attachment table is a RESOURCE CONDITION, not a bug, so the caller keeps
    /// what it had. It is `TimedOut(pending:)`'s shape — the error payload
    /// carries the live thing — and the kernel's half is what makes it honest: a
    /// refused add consumes nothing, so the wrapper's word is still good.
    ///
    /// **THE FUNNEL CONTRACT IS KEPT, AND THE REFUSAL RE-MINTS.** Every other
    /// `give` in the tree disarms BEFORE the syscall because every failure of
    /// such a syscall is a fault; this one has a STATUS outcome as well, so the
    /// body disarms first (so no path can leave two owners of one word) and the
    /// error arm builds a FRESH wrapper around the word the kernel provably did
    /// not consume. That is stronger than a conditional disarm: the value handed
    /// back is a new one, and the old husk is dead on every path.
    ///
    /// (`PipeReply.resolve`'s conditional disarm is the other shape and stays
    /// the departure — it cannot re-mint, because on ITS would-block path the
    /// caller's own binding is the thing that must survive.)
    ///
    /// It defaults to `OneShot` where `add` defaults to `Persistent`, and the
    /// asymmetry is deliberate: a caller that has given the object away has no
    /// handle left to `remove` with in the ordinary case, so the subscription
    /// that ends itself is the one that reads right. Pass `Persistent`
    /// explicitly for the attachment-owned supervisor ruling 11(c) names.
    public func give(&self, event: Event, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, Event)> {
        var owned = move event
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = EventHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, Event) = (error, Event(handle: EventHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand an Interrupt to this Waiter. Everything `give(event:)` says holds.
    public func give(&self, interrupt: Interrupt, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, Interrupt)> {
        var owned = move interrupt
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = InterruptHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, Interrupt) = (error, Interrupt(handle: InterruptHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand a Timer to this Waiter. Everything `give(event:)` says holds — and
    /// note what `Persistent` means here: a periodic timer nobody holds a handle
    /// to still fires, and its subscription ends when the Waiter does.
    public func give(&self, timer: Timer, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, Timer)> {
        var owned = move timer
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = TimerHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, Timer) = (error, Timer(handle: TimerHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Stop watching whatever is attached under `key`. A key this Waiter does
    /// not use terminates the process. Requires `WaiterRight.Attach`.
    ///
    /// THE KEY IS WHAT NAMES AN ATTACHMENT, not the waitable: detaching edits
    /// this Waiter's own table and never touches the object on the other end,
    /// so it needs no handle and no right over it. That is also what will let a
    /// stale attachment be removed once handles can be closed — at which point
    /// there is no handle left to name it with.
    public func remove(&self, key: UInt) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_waiter_remove(self.handle, key))
    }

    /// Block until an attached handle is ready, and answer with the attacher's
    /// key and what fired. Requires `WaiterRight.Wait`.
    ///
    /// Returns IMMEDIATELY when something is already ready, which is what
    /// level-triggering buys: the readiness a signal left behind is still
    /// there, so "signal then wait" and "wait then signal" have the same
    /// outcome and only the second one parks.
    ///
    /// THE ANSWER CONSUMES WHAT IT REPORTS, for an Event and a Timer alike: the
    /// record carries the accumulated word (or the fire count) and the object is
    /// emptied in the same step, so nothing is reported twice. An Interrupt is
    /// the exception and §9 is why — its readiness ends at `ack`, because the
    /// line stays masked until the device has been serviced.
    ///
    /// SEVERAL THREADS ON ONE WAITER IS A DISTRIBUTION: a ready handle wakes one
    /// of them and that thread's record carries the value, so a worker pool
    /// shares a stream of notifications with no coordination. Ownership is the
    /// other axis and it is structural — a waitable attaches to at most one
    /// Waiter, so nobody else can be watching the object you are waiting on.
    ///
    /// **A PARK CAN END IN `Err(SosStatus.Revoked)`** (sawos design 12, M4 unit
    /// 0): if this Waiter's last handle is released while you are parked on it,
    /// the kernel WAKES you with that status rather than leaving you blocked
    /// forever — nothing parked is silently doomed. No record is written on
    /// that path, so the buffer is untouched and there is nothing to decode;
    /// the handle you called through is stale from that moment, because the
    /// object it named is gone. It needs no new surface here: a revocation is a
    /// status like any other, and `checked` turns it into the error channel
    /// this method already answers with.
    ///
    /// THE BUFFER IS THIS FRAME'S, and a caller never sees it. The kernel
    /// answers a wait by copying a record into the caller's own memory (it has
    /// no allocator and nowhere to put one that would outlive the call), so
    /// somebody has to supply the bytes; supplying them HERE is what keeps a
    /// raw address out of the typed surface, exactly as the handle rule keeps
    /// raw handle words out. The stack is the right place for them: the record
    /// is four header words plus a message-sized body region since M4 unit 3, and
    /// the frame outlives the park, since the frame is the parked thread's own
    /// stack. **THE BUFFER IS MAX-SIZED AND THE COPY IS NOT** (`designs/010`
    /// ruling 11(a)): a parked waiter cannot predict which arm fires, so the
    /// buffer holds the biggest answer its attachments could give, while the
    /// kernel writes only what the delivery needs. A Timer waiter pays for the
    /// growth in frame bytes and never in copy time.
    public func wait(&self) -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
        wait_into_frame(self.handle)
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `wait`, confined to one small named helper (design 136).
/// The buffer's address never leaves it.
func wait_into_frame(handle: WaiterHandle) unsafe -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
    var buf = WaitBuffer(header: [0; WAIT_HEADER_WORDS],
                         body: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    try checked(sos_waiter_wait(handle,
                                (&var buf.header) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                wait_record_bytes() as UInt))
    return decode_wait(&buf)
}
